1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's the third most abundant metal on Earth.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Once considered so rare and precious, it was used to crown the Washington Monument.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Today it transports us, protects us, and can even help unlock the secrets of the universe.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
If you look around anywhere, you will see it being applied in just about everything we do.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Can you imagine a world without aluminum?

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,360
Find out on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:56,300
There's a good reason the world devours 165,000 tons of aluminum every day.

8
00:00:56,301 --> 00:01:02,520
and why the United States alone gobbles almost 9 billion pounds a year.

9
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,900
Modern civilization couldn't survive without it.

10
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,980
This strong, lightweight, flexible, rust-proof wonder is everywhere.

11
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:20,680
An integral part of the planes we fly, the cars we drive,

12
00:01:20,681 --> 00:01:23,220
the structures of our cities,

13
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:26,980
and the power lines carrying electricity to our homes.

14
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:33,780
But most know it best when aluminum is no thicker than a human hair.

15
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:41,220
It's found this way in over 95% of households as aluminum foil.

16
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:46,360
Impervious to light, air, and moisture,

17
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:49,620
it's perfect for preparing and preserving food.

18
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:54,080
There's nothing better for a barrier.

19
00:01:54,081 --> 00:01:59,920
Moisture protection, freshness, the deadfold characteristics that we use to wrap our products

20
00:01:59,921 --> 00:02:04,180
is just, there's nothing better.

21
00:02:04,181 --> 00:02:06,640
But how does it get so thin?

22
00:02:06,641 --> 00:02:11,480
And why is it shiny on one side and dull on the other?

23
00:02:11,481 --> 00:02:17,480
The Reynolds factory in Richmond, Virginia, maker of 340,000 pounds of aluminum foil every

24
00:02:17,481 --> 00:02:20,327
day holds the answers.

25
00:02:20,328 --> 00:02:28,480
The aluminum arrives by rail car as massive 32,000 pound coils.

26
00:02:28,481 --> 00:02:36,480
Once we receive the coils, they're unloaded off the rail car, we go into annealing ovens.

27
00:02:36,481 --> 00:02:42,480
The annealing process helps soften the metal so that we can further reduce it to the gauge

28
00:02:42,481 --> 00:02:45,340
and thickness that most people see in our standard Reynolds

29
00:02:45,341 --> 00:02:47,980
wrap products.

30
00:02:47,981 --> 00:02:51,720
Each sheet is 4 and 1 1⁄2 thousandths of an inch thick,

31
00:02:51,721 --> 00:02:53,420
about the same as a compact disc.

32
00:02:56,980 --> 00:03:01,320
Thinning the softened sheet is a job for the rolling mills.

33
00:03:01,321 --> 00:03:03,260
Since the pressure they exert on the aluminum

34
00:03:03,261 --> 00:03:07,340
could create sparks and ignite fires,

35
00:03:07,341 --> 00:03:09,840
a cooling lubricant douses the sheet as it enters.

36
00:03:09,841 --> 00:03:16,760
inside the mill the sheet passes between a pair of powered spinning rollers

37
00:03:16,761 --> 00:03:23,680
larger non-powered rollers positioned above and below them apply even pressure

38
00:03:23,681 --> 00:03:26,620
helping them flatten the sheet in a uniform manner

39
00:03:26,621 --> 00:03:31,720
one pass through the mill reduces the sheet thickness by half

40
00:03:31,721 --> 00:03:43,200
It's also getting a lot longer, as the 300,000-yard-long coiled sheet makes six passes through three different rolling mills.

41
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,020
When it finishes on our finishing mill, it's about 240 miles long because we've reduced it and increased the length.

42
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:59,280
This final pass through the finishing mill holds the secret to why aluminum foil is shiny on one side but not the other.

43
00:03:59,281 --> 00:04:04,280
So in order to support the metal and ensure we can pull it through the mill, we double

44
00:04:04,281 --> 00:04:09,780
past it. We have two sheets of metal running through the mill at the same time.

45
00:04:09,781 --> 00:04:15,520
Since the outer sides of each sheet contact the highly polished, lubricated roller surface,

46
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:17,057
they emerge shiny.

47
00:04:17,058 --> 00:04:22,780
And since the inner sides touch only each other, they emerge with a

48
00:04:22,781 --> 00:04:28,940
matte finish.

49
00:04:28,941 --> 00:04:35,380
After the double foil exits, each single layer is only 6.4 ten thousandths of an inch thick,

50
00:04:35,381 --> 00:04:38,300
six times thinner than a sheet of standard copier paper.

51
00:04:38,301 --> 00:04:44,180
In this process, we're separating the metal from the double sheet to single sheet.

52
00:04:44,181 --> 00:04:46,900
The separation occurs at this point.

53
00:04:46,901 --> 00:04:52,240
One sheet is going to the upper roll, the other sheet to the lower roll.

54
00:04:52,241 --> 00:04:57,760
As the sheets of foil separate, a set of knives behind this metal housing slices the

55
00:04:57,761 --> 00:05:01,920
rolls into six equal divisions, measuring 12 inches across.

56
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:08,920
Next, mechanical arms break apart the divisions.

57
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:12,820
Now the foil is at its final thickness and width.

58
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:19,960
Shortening each coil to the standard length

59
00:05:19,961 --> 00:05:21,500
happens at the packing facility.

60
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,840
The coil runs through a spooler

61
00:05:26,841 --> 00:05:31,000
It cuts each sheet and wraps it around a fiber core 300 times per minute.

62
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:36,920
Machines then guide the cores into cartons,

63
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:42,420
and the foil is ready to wrap food for the fridge, freezer, or grill all around the world.

64
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:46,600
But does it matter which side faces in and which side faces out?

65
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:52,320
Actually, it makes no difference whether you use the dull versus the shiny side of the foil.

66
00:05:52,321 --> 00:05:57,360
they both are going to perform the same for cooking or freezing or storing with foil.

67
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:02,020
Aluminum foil's roots stretch back over eight decades.

68
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:10,160
When metals other than aluminum were preserving the goods that fed our sweet tooths and smoking habits.

69
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:15,780
It got its origins way back in about the 1920s

70
00:06:15,781 --> 00:06:20,340
when Reynolds Metals Company was in the business of producing lead and tin foil

71
00:06:20,341 --> 00:06:22,140
that was used in cigarette packaging.

72
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:31,140
And they started wondering if possibly this protective foil could be made better and more economically by using the new metal called aluminum.

73
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:35,700
Aluminum arrived for home use in 1947.

74
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:46,820
Touted as a new kitchen miracle, Reynolds Wrap debuted as a 25-foot-long, 12-inch-wide roll packaged in pink and silver.

75
00:06:46,821 --> 00:06:53,240
Well, aluminum foil as a household foil was never made from lead or tin.

76
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:57,560
And it's kind of interesting that people still refer to it sometimes as tin foil today.

77
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:04,180
But where does the aluminum used to make foil and a million other things come from?

78
00:07:05,020 --> 00:07:06,580
It's right beneath our feet.

79
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:11,960
Beckoning to miners is the most abundant metal in the Earth's crust.

80
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:17,840
Out of every one million atoms in the ground, 82,000 are aluminum.

81
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,400
But pure aluminum doesn't exist on its own in nature.

82
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:28,380
It can be found locked away in reddish deposits of bauxite ore.

83
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:32,940
The richest deposits of the ore exist in Australia,

84
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:35,920
Brazil,

85
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:38,300
Russia,

86
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:39,520
and China.

87
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:48,960
A refining process will begin to extract the stubborn aluminum.

88
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:56,960
A caustic soda digests the ore-rich dirt into a liquid and allows the separation of its components.

89
00:07:56,961 --> 00:08:08,960
The resulting white residue is then dried into a white powder, a compound called aluminum oxide, or alumina.

90
00:08:08,961 --> 00:08:12,960
This is the stuff from which aluminum is made.

91
00:08:12,961 --> 00:08:17,960
The trick now is isolating the aluminum from the oxygen.

92
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,960
To pull that off, you need to transport the alumina to a production facility,

93
00:08:23,961 --> 00:08:28,960
like the one Alcoa operates here, near Evansville, Indiana.

94
00:08:28,961 --> 00:08:32,960
We receive a barge every day into our oar dock.

95
00:08:32,961 --> 00:08:35,960
It's got roughly 3.2 million pounds of aluminum on it,

96
00:08:35,961 --> 00:08:38,127
it and we vacuum it out of the barge.

97
00:08:38,128 --> 00:08:40,880
It's basically like a large vacuum cleaner that

98
00:08:40,881 --> 00:08:42,655
you'd use at home.

99
00:08:42,656 --> 00:08:49,460
The vacuum suctions about 300 tons per hour, emptying the barge in 10

100
00:08:49,461 --> 00:08:53,129
hours and depositing the powder onto conveyor belts.

101
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:55,880
Actually there's no smell to it at all.

102
00:08:55,881 --> 00:08:58,525
It's got a real granular feel to it.

103
00:08:58,526 --> 00:09:02,420
It's a little more coarse than what talcum powder is,

104
00:09:02,421 --> 00:09:07,120
is more like salt.

105
00:09:07,121 --> 00:09:14,580
Inside the 120-acre mega-complex, 750 pots await the powder.

106
00:09:14,581 --> 00:09:18,820
Each contains a molten salt bath.

107
00:09:18,821 --> 00:09:20,920
Every two minutes, an overhead feeder

108
00:09:20,921 --> 00:09:25,260
dumps seven pounds into one of the pots.

109
00:09:25,261 --> 00:09:28,740
As the powder begins to melt, the most critical component

110
00:09:28,741 --> 00:09:30,980
to isolating the aluminum takes charge.

111
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:38,720
Electricity, and lots of it.

112
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:43,300
The onsite coal plant delivers enough juice to the pots

113
00:09:43,301 --> 00:09:45,700
to power 300,000 homes.

114
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:50,920
The electricity flows through the copper rods

115
00:09:50,921 --> 00:09:54,220
connected to steel blocks suspended in a pot salt bath.

116
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:05,520
The aluminum oxide molecules filling the bath contain two aluminum atoms and three oxygen atoms.

117
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:10,460
The electric current breaks the bond and forces the atoms apart.

118
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:13,800
And in that process, you're making liquid metal.

119
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:15,920
The metal is a little bit denser than the bath.

120
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,860
It settles to the bottom of the pot, and then carbon dioxide bubbles are given off.

121
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:23,440
But the process isn't over yet.

122
00:10:23,441 --> 00:10:29,200
workers then have to extract the molten aluminum from the pots and turn it into a solid

123
00:10:29,201 --> 00:10:41,060
crews literally vacuum the metal out in a process we call tapping they use a large container and

124
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:44,700
it's got a spout on it we apply air to it and create a vacuum and suck the metal out of the

125
00:10:44,701 --> 00:10:53,420
bottom of the pot forklifts then transport the crucibles to a holding furnace where cranes lift

126
00:10:53,421 --> 00:11:05,840
and spill out the molten metal, then channels into casting chambers where it will solidify.

127
00:11:05,841 --> 00:11:12,300
These holding monoliths are destined to become everything from beer cans to saucepans to

128
00:11:12,301 --> 00:11:15,480
siding on your house.

129
00:11:15,481 --> 00:11:19,960
But if you thought aluminum is the only secret to all your typical household items, think

130
00:11:19,961 --> 00:11:23,340
again.

131
00:11:23,341 --> 00:11:24,041
Aluminum.

132
00:11:24,042 --> 00:11:30,800
From the kitchen to cans, its many uses have become an integral part of our daily lives.

133
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:35,700
Not bad for a metal that's definitely the new kid on the block.

134
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:40,080
Well, the one thing to keep in mind is that aluminum is a fairly new metal.

135
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:44,900
Bronze, lead, iron, copper have been around for centuries.

136
00:11:44,901 --> 00:11:53,900
Aluminum was first actually discovered as an element by chemist Sir Davy in 1808.

137
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:57,900
But discovering this unique metal was one thing.

138
00:11:57,901 --> 00:12:00,900
Divorcing it from its ore compound was another.

139
00:12:00,901 --> 00:12:03,900
Throughout most of the 19th century,

140
00:12:03,901 --> 00:12:09,900
a variety of chemical processes could only generate impure samples in minute quantities.

141
00:12:09,901 --> 00:12:19,923
quantities. Aluminum was so rare, it was nearly twice as expensive as gold.

142
00:12:19,924 --> 00:12:21,660
In the 1880s,

143
00:12:21,661 --> 00:12:27,400
when advances in processing helped reduce its price, it was the fitting choice for engineers

144
00:12:27,401 --> 00:12:35,900
completing construction of the Washington Monument, who sought a precious capstone for their masterwork.

145
00:12:35,901 --> 00:12:42,080
The aluminum pyramid they placed atop the marble spire weighed 100 ounces, at the time

146
00:12:42,081 --> 00:12:46,280
the largest piece of aluminum ever cast.

147
00:12:46,281 --> 00:12:54,960
Two years later in 1886 came the discovery that made aluminum accessible and inexpensive.

148
00:12:54,961 --> 00:13:00,640
Two scientists working independently, Frenchman Paul LaRue and American Charles Martin Hall,

149
00:13:00,641 --> 00:13:06,700
upon the chemical-electric extraction process still in use today.

150
00:13:06,701 --> 00:13:11,580
Aluminum's market price plummeted to less than a dollar per pound.

151
00:13:11,581 --> 00:13:19,040
The breakthrough unshackled the metal and triggered the rise of revolutionary new industries.

152
00:13:19,041 --> 00:13:24,680
Today one of those industries crafts the aluminum that helps lift this Goliath double-decker

153
00:13:24,681 --> 00:13:27,360
jet.

154
00:13:27,361 --> 00:13:32,740
The Airbus A380 is the largest commercial aircraft in the world.

155
00:13:32,741 --> 00:13:40,920
It stretches 10 feet longer and towers 16 feet taller than a Boeing 747.

156
00:13:40,921 --> 00:13:45,960
It weighs in at a whopping 1.2 million pounds.

157
00:13:45,961 --> 00:13:54,400
And like most commercial aircraft, 65% of that bulk is aluminum.

158
00:13:54,401 --> 00:13:58,020
It's a no-brainer that aluminum's light weight makes it a perfect construction material for

159
00:13:58,021 --> 00:14:00,480
planes.

160
00:14:00,481 --> 00:14:04,020
But more important is its flexibility.

161
00:14:04,021 --> 00:14:08,780
Aluminum is ideally suited for flying more than any other material.

162
00:14:08,781 --> 00:14:14,220
It's important that the material be able to withstand and give and take in those pressurizations

163
00:14:14,221 --> 00:14:21,320
and depressurizations that occur thousands of times over the life cycle of an airplane.

164
00:14:21,321 --> 00:14:27,880
The wings of the A380, like any airplane, suffer constant stress.

165
00:14:27,881 --> 00:14:32,340
On the ground, they sag from their own weight.

166
00:14:32,341 --> 00:14:39,800
In the sky, the airflow required for lift pushes the wings in the opposite direction.

167
00:14:39,801 --> 00:14:45,920
The A380's pliable wings span over 260 feet and are comprised of the largest pieces of

168
00:14:45,921 --> 00:14:50,280
aluminum on the plane.

169
00:14:50,281 --> 00:14:58,280
Ten separate sheets case the surface of each wing, five on top and five underneath.

170
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:03,360
They cover a series of aluminum ribs and spars, which form the skeleton needed to support

171
00:15:03,361 --> 00:15:09,520
the wing's length.

172
00:15:09,521 --> 00:15:15,780
The wing skins for the A380 are among the thousands of aluminum parts made here at Alcoa's Davenport

173
00:15:15,781 --> 00:15:19,280
Works Mill in Iowa.

174
00:15:19,281 --> 00:15:25,180
The plant is large enough to contain a golf course.

175
00:15:25,181 --> 00:15:34,000
Mill workers craft wing sections up to 112 feet long and weighing over 11,000 pounds.

176
00:15:34,001 --> 00:15:40,960
They begin with enormous 18-inch thick, 70-inch wide aluminum alloy blocks.

177
00:15:40,961 --> 00:15:52,520
The task of transforming each into a wing skin begins with this beastly rolling metal,

178
00:15:52,521 --> 00:16:00,020
measuring over 18 feet across, the widest in the world.

179
00:16:00,021 --> 00:16:04,720
Just like the raw sheets that become aluminum foil, the metal will get thinner and thinner

180
00:16:04,721 --> 00:16:10,240
and longer and longer until it is about one inch thick.

181
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,520
Saws then cut each flattened plate to various shapes,

182
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:19,880
depending on where they'll be positioned on the wing.

183
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,760
Finally, twin milling heads refine and taper the pieces as necessary

184
00:16:28,761 --> 00:16:30,660
by skinning the plate surface.

185
00:16:30,661 --> 00:16:33,450
We use 30-inch diameter cutter.

186
00:16:33,451 --> 00:16:39,660
It will generate this machine pattern on here that looks like swirls.

187
00:16:42,760 --> 00:16:47,660
The completed wing skins are now ready for assembly by Airbus and other aircraft manufacturers.

188
00:16:50,700 --> 00:16:54,400
Flying with anything but aluminum seems almost inconceivable today.

189
00:16:54,401 --> 00:17:02,260
Yet plane-making pioneers fashioned the bodies of their crafts out of fabric-covered wood.

190
00:17:04,260 --> 00:17:07,200
But wood had rocked and splintered.

191
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:11,000
Seeking a more durable material following World War I,

192
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,600
German designer Hugo Junkers was among the first to build planes sheathed in aluminum alloy.

193
00:17:16,601 --> 00:17:29,120
In 1935, the Douglas DC-3 and its all-aluminum body ushered in the age of commercial flight.

194
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:35,460
Sporting longer and larger wings than its predecessors, it could fly higher and faster.

195
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:41,800
It could also carry more fuel, minimizing refueling stops.

196
00:17:41,801 --> 00:17:48,580
The duration of a coast-to-coast flight fell from an agonizing 26 hours to less than 18.

197
00:17:50,740 --> 00:17:57,980
And today, aluminum carries commercial travelers through the air for more than 800 billion miles every year.

198
00:17:59,060 --> 00:18:02,680
But plans aren't the only form of transportation revolutionized by aluminum.

199
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:07,040
Our favorite four-wheelers contain over 500 pounds of it.

200
00:18:07,041 --> 00:18:15,160
For over 100 years, aluminum has been used to manufacture automobiles.

201
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:25,620
In fact, Carl Benz, who co-founded the world-famous Mercedes-Benz company, constructed the world's first aluminum car engine in 1901.

202
00:18:27,840 --> 00:18:32,100
It's estimated that car companies use 20% of the aluminum mined each year.

203
00:18:33,820 --> 00:18:36,060
And proportionally less and less steel.

204
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:38,040
Why?

205
00:18:38,041 --> 00:18:42,040
Well, for starters, it saves gas.

206
00:18:42,041 --> 00:18:51,040
Aluminum is significantly lighter than steel, and it gives you somewhere between 30 and

207
00:18:51,041 --> 00:18:54,040
50 percent mass reduction compared to steel.

208
00:18:54,041 --> 00:19:00,040
And the general ratio is if you take 10 percent of the mass out of a vehicle, you can get

209
00:19:00,041 --> 00:19:06,040
up to about 8 percent of improvement in fuel economy.

210
00:19:06,041 --> 00:19:13,040
Automakers prefer aluminum because it's easier to melt and to shape.

211
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:20,040
That's a big plus when it comes to casting the parts for the engine.

212
00:19:20,041 --> 00:19:25,040
That represents much of a car's weight.

213
00:19:25,041 --> 00:19:28,040
What we've done here is take two crucibles.

214
00:19:28,041 --> 00:19:32,040
We filled one with aluminum and the other one with chunks of steel.

215
00:19:32,041 --> 00:19:41,040
We put both of these crucibles in a furnace and we set the furnace at 800 degrees centigrade.

216
00:19:41,041 --> 00:19:47,280
You can see that the aluminum is melted and the steel is still chunks of steel that are

217
00:19:47,281 --> 00:19:52,720
sitting there, maybe a bit red hot, but nevertheless not melted.

218
00:19:52,721 --> 00:19:56,600
This is one of the most critical and important attributes of aluminum, that it can melt at

219
00:19:56,601 --> 00:20:00,340
at low temperatures so we can melt it

220
00:20:00,341 --> 00:20:03,380
and pour it into cavities to make castings.

221
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:07,480
While this fluid wonder metal is helping cars

222
00:20:07,481 --> 00:20:09,840
lose weight under their hoods,

223
00:20:09,841 --> 00:20:12,820
it's also helping them get their bodies into shape.

224
00:20:12,821 --> 00:20:14,620
It also allows you to make a lot of shapes

225
00:20:14,621 --> 00:20:16,260
that you couldn't make otherwise.

226
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:22,160
One way GM shapes its aluminum parts is with water

227
00:20:22,161 --> 00:20:24,120
in a process known as hydroforming.

228
00:20:24,121 --> 00:20:30,160
The reason we tube hydroform is we're able to form it in any particular shape that we

229
00:20:30,161 --> 00:20:33,280
need it without welding sections together.

230
00:20:33,281 --> 00:20:40,260
The hydroforming process allows us to make one part and it's proven to be higher durability

231
00:20:40,261 --> 00:20:46,440
than you get out of two pieces welded together.

232
00:20:46,441 --> 00:20:51,640
The water-shaped tubes will become part of the frame of a Corvette.

233
00:20:51,641 --> 00:20:55,140
Once a tube enters the die, high-capacity pumps

234
00:20:55,141 --> 00:20:59,780
flood it with up to 100 gallons of water.

235
00:20:59,781 --> 00:21:01,880
The resulting pressure helps the aluminum form

236
00:21:01,881 --> 00:21:06,020
into the precise shapes of the die.

237
00:21:06,021 --> 00:21:10,020
It's all over in less than two minutes.

238
00:21:10,021 --> 00:21:13,260
I'm sitting in the Corvette body structure

239
00:21:13,261 --> 00:21:16,860
that contains aluminum hydroformed rails.

240
00:21:16,861 --> 00:21:20,040
These aluminum rails replaced steel rails

241
00:21:20,041 --> 00:21:22,200
that serve the same function but were heavier.

242
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:29,340
This enables us to produce a Corvette that is lighter

243
00:21:29,341 --> 00:21:31,620
and has better zero to 60 time

244
00:21:31,621 --> 00:21:33,380
than you would get with the steel version.

245
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:38,160
The pressure to shape aluminum car parts

246
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:39,900
isn't restricted to water.

247
00:21:39,901 --> 00:21:43,760
You can also use air with what is known as QPF.

248
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:47,800
It stands for quick plastic forming,

249
00:21:47,801 --> 00:21:49,500
which is not plastic.

250
00:21:49,501 --> 00:21:54,600
We're working with aluminum, but we take aluminum to an elevated temperature state in order

251
00:21:54,601 --> 00:21:59,420
to form parts and shapes that we can't normally form.

252
00:21:59,421 --> 00:22:04,140
In this operation, we're making the Cadillac STS trunk lid.

253
00:22:04,141 --> 00:22:07,900
The inner panel is just now coming out of the press fully formed.

254
00:22:07,901 --> 00:22:11,060
What's coming in is the preheated aluminum sheet.

255
00:22:11,061 --> 00:22:17,740
As soon as that die gets closed, we use air pressure to force that aluminum up against

256
00:22:17,741 --> 00:22:21,280
that steel dye cavity that gives us our shape.

257
00:22:23,580 --> 00:22:26,340
QPF Technology fashioned aluminum body panels

258
00:22:26,341 --> 00:22:28,600
for GM's fuel cell-powered concept car.

259
00:22:29,960 --> 00:22:30,800
The sequel.

260
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:37,180
Whether powered by hydrogen, battery, or hybrid engines,

261
00:22:37,940 --> 00:22:40,940
future vehicles must become lighter than ever to be competitive.

262
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:45,820
When you put a lot of money into the powertrain,

263
00:22:45,940 --> 00:22:47,400
like you need to do in those vehicles,

264
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:50,460
it doesn't make sense to pull around a steel cage.

265
00:22:51,540 --> 00:22:54,880
A wood bat is the foundation of professional baseball

266
00:22:54,881 --> 00:22:57,840
and has been since before the days of the babe.

267
00:23:01,360 --> 00:23:05,120
To maintain that tradition, pro players can't use anything else.

268
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:10,900
But aspiring pros aren't required to use wood.

269
00:23:12,580 --> 00:23:15,460
In fact, more than 90% of all bats sold today

270
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:17,580
are made not from wood.

271
00:23:17,740 --> 00:23:19,400
but aluminum.

272
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:21,740
The reasons are obvious.

273
00:23:22,940 --> 00:23:25,240
First, bats made from aluminum are lighter,

274
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:29,120
enabling players to generate more bat speed as they swing.

275
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:33,660
But unlike wood,

276
00:23:34,360 --> 00:23:36,060
aluminum can be strategically balanced

277
00:23:36,061 --> 00:23:37,300
along the length of the bat,

278
00:23:38,500 --> 00:23:40,060
allowing it to channel vibrations

279
00:23:40,061 --> 00:23:42,080
and transfer energy more efficiently.

280
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:45,700
As a result, on contact,

281
00:23:45,701 --> 00:23:48,000
baseballs fly up to 20 feet farther.

282
00:23:49,440 --> 00:23:53,360
And the bat's sweet spot, the ideal hit zone, is larger.

283
00:23:54,440 --> 00:23:57,100
They debuted at the collegiate level in 1974

284
00:23:57,101 --> 00:24:01,280
and forever altered the nature of the game.

285
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:07,320
By 1976, the batting averages for NCAA had climbed 30 points.

286
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:10,400
So there was a big difference in terms of

287
00:24:10,401 --> 00:24:12,420
just simply putting the ball in play.

288
00:24:12,421 --> 00:24:16,620
The Anderson Bat Company in Orange County, California,

289
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:19,960
one of the few remaining American bat makers,

290
00:24:19,961 --> 00:24:23,160
crafts 300 aluminum bats every day.

291
00:24:27,160 --> 00:24:29,640
The simple shape of the aluminum bat

292
00:24:29,641 --> 00:24:32,080
masks the precise science behind its creation.

293
00:24:32,081 --> 00:24:36,720
The aluminum has the strength-to-weight ratio

294
00:24:36,721 --> 00:24:39,780
that is probably the most advantageous of any of the metals.

295
00:24:39,781 --> 00:24:47,920
Anderson makes its top-of-the-line bat from an aluminum alloy with a touch of zinc that adds toughness and durability.

296
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:54,620
The process begins with 17-foot-long hollow aluminum tubes.

297
00:24:58,380 --> 00:25:05,320
An operator saws the tubes to lengths ranging from 22 to 26 inches, depending on the final bat model.

298
00:25:05,321 --> 00:25:14,240
Then it's up to a machine called the rotary swagger to reshape each tube into a precisely

299
00:25:14,241 --> 00:25:15,680
molded and balanced bat.

300
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:25,600
The aluminum fits over a mandrel bar as it enters the swagger between a twin pair of

301
00:25:25,601 --> 00:25:26,301
dies.

302
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:31,860
As the tapered die halves rotate, they open out.

303
00:25:31,861 --> 00:25:35,800
A series of spinning rollers positioned around the perimeter

304
00:25:35,801 --> 00:25:38,660
shove the dies back and close them.

305
00:25:41,100 --> 00:25:45,320
The rotation opens and closes the dies 1,500 times per minute.

306
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:49,380
As the aluminum feeds into the swagger,

307
00:25:50,040 --> 00:25:52,640
it's forced to take the shape of the narrowing die cavity.

308
00:25:55,920 --> 00:25:59,140
The aluminum needs three passes through the rotary swagger.

309
00:25:59,141 --> 00:26:03,300
Each pass thins the wall and elongates the tube.

310
00:26:04,620 --> 00:26:06,740
A final pass tapers the handle.

311
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:09,900
This is the cut stock.

312
00:26:10,020 --> 00:26:13,320
This is what you saw earlier, and it's 26 inches long.

313
00:26:13,900 --> 00:26:15,660
This one is a first pass.

314
00:26:15,740 --> 00:26:17,420
It's the thinning stage of it.

315
00:26:18,220 --> 00:26:21,120
This is a second thinning stage,

316
00:26:21,160 --> 00:26:24,980
and you can see that as we go and thin, the tube gets longer.

317
00:26:24,981 --> 00:26:35,160
and then this is the final pass and well this is 26 this is 41 inches long

318
00:26:35,161 --> 00:26:40,280
these bats may now be expertly shaped and balanced but before they can punish

319
00:26:40,281 --> 00:26:47,580
baseballs they have to be hardened this happens just a few miles away at a

320
00:26:47,581 --> 00:26:54,440
facility where a hot bath waits to heat treat the aluminum the bath doesn't hold

321
00:26:54,441 --> 00:27:00,320
hot water, but rather sodium nitrate.

322
00:27:00,321 --> 00:27:04,720
Unlike water, the salt solution won't corrode the aluminum.

323
00:27:04,721 --> 00:27:09,640
Heat treat is a two-step process, an elevated temperature for a certain amount of time and

324
00:27:09,641 --> 00:27:13,200
then a rapid cooling called a quench.

325
00:27:13,201 --> 00:27:17,800
It's kind of exciting because you go from the salt bath to a water tank and you have

326
00:27:17,801 --> 00:27:20,880
to do it with less than 15 seconds.

327
00:27:20,881 --> 00:27:26,800
A rapid cooling following the elevated temperature soak creates microscopic particles in the

328
00:27:26,801 --> 00:27:32,340
aluminum, which strengthen the metal.

329
00:27:32,341 --> 00:27:36,660
After the bats bake in an oven to preserve their hardness and shape, an inspector checks

330
00:27:36,661 --> 00:27:40,320
each one to see if it's a hit or an error.

331
00:27:40,321 --> 00:27:45,080
What we're looking for is a range.

332
00:27:45,081 --> 00:27:47,380
If it's too hard, the aluminum can crack.

333
00:27:47,381 --> 00:27:50,420
And if it's too soft, obviously it will dent.

334
00:27:52,780 --> 00:27:56,800
After the inspection, workers at another facility add the graphics and color.

335
00:28:00,580 --> 00:28:04,200
And workers back at Anderson Bat Company apply the finishing touches.

336
00:28:05,720 --> 00:28:10,140
An end on the handle, cap, and the grip.

337
00:28:11,400 --> 00:28:15,020
How does this finely crafted aluminum bat compare to one made of wood?

338
00:28:15,820 --> 00:28:20,780
What we're measuring is the velocity of the ball off of the bat from the tee.

339
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:27,300
And the radar gun actually picks up the very fastest point of the ball between the tee and the gun.

340
00:28:28,160 --> 00:28:29,740
And we're going to start with the wood bat.

341
00:28:32,060 --> 00:28:34,300
Okay, that's 86 miles an hour.

342
00:28:36,620 --> 00:28:37,320
85.

343
00:28:40,260 --> 00:28:42,160
And now, the aluminum bat.

344
00:28:44,080 --> 00:28:44,780
91.

345
00:28:45,020 --> 00:28:47,720
91.

346
00:28:48,980 --> 00:28:49,680
Wow.

347
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:54,120
If, say, this point right here is my ideal hit zone,

348
00:28:54,300 --> 00:28:55,680
where I'm going to get my best performance,

349
00:28:56,200 --> 00:28:59,360
with an aluminum bat, the size of that hit zone

350
00:28:59,361 --> 00:29:02,380
is going to be larger than with a wood bat.

351
00:29:02,680 --> 00:29:06,860
So, say, the wood bat is going to be the size of a baseball.

352
00:29:07,640 --> 00:29:09,340
Your aluminum bat is going to be double that.

353
00:29:09,380 --> 00:29:10,660
It's going to be the size of two baseballs.

354
00:29:10,940 --> 00:29:13,420
So, the chances of me getting better performance

355
00:29:13,421 --> 00:29:16,880
when I miss it, a bat is going to increase as opposed to using a wood bat.

356
00:29:20,040 --> 00:29:23,360
Will aluminum bats ever graduate from college to the pros?

357
00:29:25,320 --> 00:29:26,340
Don't hold your breath.

358
00:29:26,780 --> 00:29:30,500
Baseball is a very traditional sport, and wood, the crack of the bat,

359
00:29:30,660 --> 00:29:33,740
and all of that is very much a part of the fabric of baseball.

360
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:39,400
At the same time, if you were to put an aluminum bat in the hands of professional players,

361
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:41,780
you would have to change all of the records.

362
00:29:41,781 --> 00:29:43,040
They'd have to be asterisk.

363
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:48,420
Ball players aren't the only ones reaching new heights with aluminum.

364
00:29:48,421 --> 00:29:54,420
A new telescope called the Giant Magellan, the world's largest,

365
00:29:54,421 --> 00:30:00,420
is planned to begin peering skyward from an observatory in Chile sometime in the next few years.

366
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:07,420
A cluster of seven mirrors, more than 80 feet in diameter,

367
00:30:07,421 --> 00:30:11,420
will redirect so much light from the heavens to astronomers' eyes,

368
00:30:11,421 --> 00:30:18,420
It will produce images up to ten times sharper than the Hubble Space Telescope.

369
00:30:18,421 --> 00:30:27,420
It will allow astronomers to study newly discovered black holes, stars and galaxies.

370
00:30:27,421 --> 00:30:36,420
But the giant Magellan wouldn't see anything without aluminum coating each mirror.

371
00:30:36,421 --> 00:30:42,460
Your mirror at home uses a layer of silver, and it's actually slightly more reflective

372
00:30:42,461 --> 00:30:45,540
than aluminum.

373
00:30:45,541 --> 00:30:50,680
But astronomers use aluminum for their telescopes because it's more durable and less expensive

374
00:30:50,681 --> 00:30:54,680
to maintain.

375
00:30:54,681 --> 00:30:58,660
Making aluminum-coated mirrors for telescopes isn't as simple as dipping a brush in buckets

376
00:30:58,661 --> 00:31:04,040
of aluminum paint and slapping it on glass.

377
00:31:04,041 --> 00:31:10,920
Just ask the scientists at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center in Maryland.

378
00:31:10,921 --> 00:31:16,680
The setting for the transformation of glass into mirror is a vacuum chamber.

379
00:31:16,681 --> 00:31:24,720
The process of coating the mirror demands that the environment not be contaminated.

380
00:31:24,721 --> 00:31:29,360
We actually evacuate most of the air out of the chamber.

381
00:31:29,361 --> 00:31:33,980
So the cleaner the chamber is, the cleaner the coating is going to be.

382
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:37,820
With the glass in place, it's time to add the aluminum.

383
00:31:37,821 --> 00:31:43,300
These staples are 99.999 pure aluminum.

384
00:31:43,301 --> 00:31:48,160
We use these as the base for the aluminum coatings.

385
00:31:48,161 --> 00:31:54,540
We put them by hand on the tungsten filaments to prepare for the coating process.

386
00:31:54,541 --> 00:32:03,960
The lid lowers, sealing the chamber, and a pump removes virtually all the air.

387
00:32:03,961 --> 00:32:08,880
An electric current heats the filaments, melting the staples and removing any lingering impurities

388
00:32:08,881 --> 00:32:11,880
in the aluminum.

389
00:32:11,881 --> 00:32:15,960
Once the aluminum is melted, this is the way it looks on the filament.

390
00:32:15,961 --> 00:32:23,000
So you can see that the aluminum is no longer a hard staple, but it's actually wetted along

391
00:32:23,001 --> 00:32:25,580
the coil of the filament.

392
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:32,200
Next, a second stronger electric current passes through the filaments.

393
00:32:32,201 --> 00:32:35,960
In a blinding flash, the aluminum vaporizes.

394
00:32:35,961 --> 00:32:41,700
The hot aluminum gas rises and condenses on the cooler glass surface.

395
00:32:41,701 --> 00:32:49,660
The deposited layer is 1,500 times thinner than a human hair.

396
00:32:49,661 --> 00:32:53,960
But as shiny as the aluminum coating is, it faces a host of enemies.

397
00:32:53,961 --> 00:33:00,660
Over time, moisture and pollen and bugs and things like that will get on the optical surfaces

398
00:33:00,661 --> 00:33:02,420
and they'll start etching into the aluminum,

399
00:33:02,940 --> 00:33:04,700
and they degrade the coating over time.

400
00:33:05,300 --> 00:33:07,780
The only way to restore an aluminum-coated mirror

401
00:33:07,781 --> 00:33:09,680
is to remove the corrupted coating

402
00:33:09,681 --> 00:33:11,300
and replace it with a fresh one.

403
00:33:13,420 --> 00:33:15,540
It's a major event for observatories

404
00:33:15,541 --> 00:33:18,420
and a necessity about every two years.

405
00:33:21,060 --> 00:33:23,180
First, technicians must remove the mirror

406
00:33:23,181 --> 00:33:24,380
from the telescope's housing.

407
00:33:26,260 --> 00:33:29,180
Stripping off the old coating starts with soap and water.

408
00:33:30,660 --> 00:33:37,660
Chemical solvents then eat away the aluminum coating to reveal the underlying glass base.

409
00:33:37,661 --> 00:33:44,120
Next, paper towels are used to clean and dry the glass before a vacuum chamber lowers into

410
00:33:44,121 --> 00:33:51,600
place and deposits the shiny aluminum.

411
00:33:51,601 --> 00:33:58,840
Bugs and dirt are to worry for the orbiting 95-inch aluminum coated mirror on the Hubble.

412
00:33:58,841 --> 00:34:02,040
NASA engineers have to guard it against an entirely different threat.

413
00:34:03,120 --> 00:34:06,520
You go up in space and you have extreme differentials.

414
00:34:06,521 --> 00:34:09,700
If it's facing the sun, if it's nighttime,

415
00:34:10,420 --> 00:34:13,380
you're talking hundreds of degrees in variation,

416
00:34:13,700 --> 00:34:17,420
and that'll change the contour of the mirror.

417
00:34:19,180 --> 00:34:20,620
Trying to lick the problem,

418
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:23,740
NASA scientists have developed a new kind of aluminum mirror

419
00:34:23,741 --> 00:34:25,060
without a glass base.

420
00:34:25,061 --> 00:34:34,520
You can use the aluminum to make your mirror and the mounting structure all out of the same material.

421
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:44,040
If the temperature changes, then your mirror and your mounting structure then shrinks or expands by the same amount.

422
00:34:44,220 --> 00:34:51,200
So you don't get distortions and stresses that will twist the mirror and destroy its imaging properties.

423
00:34:51,201 --> 00:34:58,200
The challenge for NASA's engineers is to turn pure aluminum discs like this one into mirrors.

424
00:34:58,201 --> 00:35:03,200
A diamond-tipped lathe is the answer.

425
00:35:03,201 --> 00:35:10,200
It will dig in just below the surface and, in a uniform slice, begin to smooth the aluminum to a natural shine.

426
00:35:14,200 --> 00:35:19,200
During the procedure, a paint thinner solution sprays away debris.

427
00:35:19,201 --> 00:35:26,200
It also cools the diamond tip, which heats up as it carves into the metal.

428
00:35:26,201 --> 00:35:33,920
And after just two minutes, a final polishing treatment will smooth out leftover microscopic

429
00:35:33,921 --> 00:35:36,400
imperfections.

430
00:35:36,401 --> 00:35:41,200
NASA scientists speculate that pure aluminum mirrors like this could someday replace the

431
00:35:41,201 --> 00:35:44,100
more common aluminum coated glass mirrors.

432
00:35:44,101 --> 00:35:51,100
Of course, NASA's love affair with aluminum isn't just limited to mirrors.

433
00:35:51,101 --> 00:35:57,100
Anything you want to lift off the surface of the Earth, you're fighting gravity.

434
00:35:57,101 --> 00:36:01,100
So you want to look for materials that are very lightweight and strong and take advantage

435
00:36:01,101 --> 00:36:04,100
of other properties.

436
00:36:04,101 --> 00:36:10,100
Reducing the mass to take it to orbit is a big deal.

437
00:36:10,101 --> 00:36:12,580
Every pound, every ounce counts.

438
00:36:13,080 --> 00:36:17,760
So aluminum has a perfect combination of many of the characteristics we look for.

439
00:36:18,780 --> 00:36:19,780
It is very lightweight.

440
00:36:20,300 --> 00:36:23,800
It's malleable, meaning that you can work with it easier.

441
00:36:24,080 --> 00:36:25,080
It doesn't corrode.

442
00:36:25,860 --> 00:36:26,560
It's easy.

443
00:36:26,600 --> 00:36:31,160
If you look around anywhere in NASA, you will see it being applied in just about everything we do.

444
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:35,600
And it's been that way for over five decades.

445
00:36:35,601 --> 00:36:41,640
It has been one of those materials that actually have made many of the things that we're doing now possible.

446
00:36:43,980 --> 00:36:47,160
As aluminum continues to help us explore new worlds,

447
00:36:47,820 --> 00:36:52,740
it could also better protect us in extreme weather events.

448
00:36:56,640 --> 00:37:02,520
There are still few measures to help prevent total destruction when an extreme weather event arises in your town.

449
00:37:02,521 --> 00:37:04,720
Tornadoes.

450
00:37:05,260 --> 00:37:09,420
In the Midwest and plains reaching 219 miles per hour.

451
00:37:12,540 --> 00:37:17,240
Costing the United States an average of $2.5 million annually in recovery.

452
00:37:20,100 --> 00:37:25,140
And in southern hurricane-prone states, a Category 5 event like Hurricane Harvey

453
00:37:25,141 --> 00:37:29,260
resulted in approximately $125 billion in damages.

454
00:37:29,261 --> 00:37:35,740
But these homes and buildings may have fared better had they been clad in another form of

455
00:37:35,741 --> 00:37:42,460
aluminum produced by Canada's CMAT technologies. They call it aluminum foam.

456
00:37:42,461 --> 00:37:48,280
Its unique sponge-like structure could prove to be a lifesaver around the globe.

457
00:37:48,281 --> 00:37:53,980
The foam basically absorbs the shockwave so that there isn't such a severe impact.

458
00:37:53,981 --> 00:37:58,420
Aluminum foam's key ingredient is nothing more than air.

459
00:37:58,421 --> 00:38:04,100
But the real trick to making it isn't injecting air bubbles into the aluminum.

460
00:38:04,101 --> 00:38:08,740
It's keeping the bubbles intact once they're inside.

461
00:38:08,741 --> 00:38:14,840
Our material is molten aluminum with ceramic particles in it.

462
00:38:14,841 --> 00:38:17,740
Those particles stabilize the

463
00:38:17,741 --> 00:38:19,855
bubbles, in other words stop them from popping.

464
00:38:19,856 --> 00:38:22,060
A good example of this is if you've ever tried to

465
00:38:22,061 --> 00:38:26,740
use dried cocoa powder and mix it into milk.

466
00:38:26,741 --> 00:38:28,360
And as you're doing it, you see this froth

467
00:38:28,361 --> 00:38:31,900
forming on the surface with the dried cocoa on the bubbles.

468
00:38:31,901 --> 00:38:35,080
The bubbles are stable.

469
00:38:35,081 --> 00:38:37,380
C-MAT begins its production process

470
00:38:37,381 --> 00:38:39,780
by melting down aluminum bars already

471
00:38:39,781 --> 00:38:43,180
containing the ceramic particles.

472
00:38:43,181 --> 00:38:46,220
The furnace holding material spills a scalding stream

473
00:38:46,221 --> 00:38:49,360
into a channel, leading to a receptacle called the foaming

474
00:38:49,361 --> 00:38:50,061
box.

475
00:38:52,060 --> 00:38:57,060
Now it's time for the all-important air.

476
00:38:57,061 --> 00:39:07,860
Inside the foaming box, a nozzle injects air creating bubbles in the molten concoction.

477
00:39:07,861 --> 00:39:14,220
More air creates a less dense and lighter final product.

478
00:39:14,221 --> 00:39:21,060
A propeller at the end of the nozzle keeps the ceramic particles evenly distributed.

479
00:39:21,061 --> 00:39:25,200
The bubbles rise and as they reach the surface, begin to cool and harden.

480
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:32,900
When it gets to the top of the foaming box, it's already starting to solidify.

481
00:39:32,901 --> 00:39:36,560
So it curves up onto the belt and goes into the press.

482
00:39:36,780 --> 00:39:43,660
And that takes a lot of the heat out very quickly to solidify the cells and give you the solid panel.

483
00:39:46,540 --> 00:39:50,080
The emerging half-inch thick panels measure four by eight feet.

484
00:39:51,060 --> 00:39:54,460
Solid aluminum this size could weigh more than 300 pounds.

485
00:39:56,360 --> 00:39:59,380
But each of these panels weighs only about 30 pounds.

486
00:40:03,100 --> 00:40:06,560
Foamed aluminum can also be injected into a three-dimensional cast.

487
00:40:07,740 --> 00:40:12,420
What looks like a heavy chunk of metal is actually light enough to float in water.

488
00:40:14,540 --> 00:40:17,100
A view of the casting skeleton shows why.

489
00:40:17,101 --> 00:40:24,220
In the x-ray machine, we have a part that from the outside looks like a solid aluminum casting.

490
00:40:24,700 --> 00:40:26,300
We're looking right on the edge of the part.

491
00:40:26,400 --> 00:40:30,740
You can see a dark line here where we're actually looking down the edge of the casting.

492
00:40:30,860 --> 00:40:33,240
But if I rotate this, you can see the cellular structure.

493
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:37,000
There's a lot more air there than there is solid material.

494
00:40:37,720 --> 00:40:41,080
The cellular structure allows it to collapse and absorb energy.

495
00:40:41,081 --> 00:40:49,280
Imagine each one of these little bubbles is breaking, these cells is breaking as the part

496
00:40:49,281 --> 00:40:53,580
is crushed and there's energy being absorbed by every one of those walls collapsing.

497
00:40:53,581 --> 00:40:58,440
One of the applications for this product is crash boxes in automobiles.

498
00:40:58,441 --> 00:41:04,720
Crash box is the element in a bumper system which absorbs the energy in a low impact crash.

499
00:41:04,721 --> 00:41:10,060
We take a normal hollow aluminum extrusion that might form that crash box and we insert

500
00:41:10,061 --> 00:41:15,140
aluminum foam inside the product and then crush it as it would be crushed in a crash.

501
00:41:15,400 --> 00:41:16,752
It does two things.

502
00:41:16,753 --> 00:41:22,300
It absorbs the energy and it also forces the aluminum extrusion to create many

503
00:41:22,301 --> 00:41:25,712
folds and every one of those folds absorbs energy.

504
00:41:25,713 --> 00:41:28,920
Aluminum foam is just the latest incarnation of

505
00:41:28,921 --> 00:41:30,607
this invaluable metal.

506
00:41:30,608 --> 00:41:36,360
A world without aluminum, perhaps I'm biased, but for me it's unimaginable.

507
00:41:36,361 --> 00:41:44,020
A century before it helped carry man to the moon, Jules Verne hinted at its vast potential,

508
00:41:44,760 --> 00:41:50,820
describing it as having the lightness of silver, the indestructibility of gold, the tenacity

509
00:41:50,821 --> 00:41:52,980
of iron, and the lightness of glass.

510
00:41:53,720 --> 00:41:58,820
But even that great visionary couldn't have foreseen the scope of aluminum's many modern

511
00:41:58,821 --> 00:41:59,521
applications.

